Introduction
\label{sec:motivation}

Recently, large language models (LLMs) have shown impressive performance in a range of natural language processing tasks<|CIT-MASK|>. However, fine-tuning pre-trained language models (PLMs) is computationally and memory-intensive.

To mitigate this, \textit{parameter-efficient tuning} (PET) methods have been developed to fine-tune a small number of (extra) model parameters instead of the entire model<|CIT-MASK|>.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/heterolora.pdf}
\caption{An illustration of the HeteroLoRA search space in a Transformer model. Given a fixed number of trainable parameters, HeteroLoRA finds an efficient heterogeneous LoRA configuration for a model on a specific task. Each of the standard LoRA module and LoRA-adapted shortcut can be enabled or disabled.}
\label{fig: heterolora}
\vspace{-15pt}
\end{figure}

Low-rank adaptation (LoRA)<|CIT-MASK|> is now the de-facto PET method. LoRA injects two low-rank matrices $A\in \mathbb{R}^{r\times d_\text{in}}$ and $B\in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{out}\times r}$ with rank $r\ll\min(d_\text{in}, d_\text{out})$, to update the pre-trained weights $W \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\text{out}\times d_\text{in}}$.
Unlike full fine-tuning, LoRA updates only the injected $A$ and $B$ with the pre-trained weights $W$ unchanged. After fine-tuning, the update weights $\Delta W = BA$ fuse back to the pre-trained weights $W'=W+AB$, incurring no additional latency. LoRA achieves performance levels similar to full fine-tuning while drastically reducing memory usage.
We identify the following limitations of LoRA.
\begin{itemize}
\item Existing methods configure LoRA modules within a model uniformly with the same rank $r$, thus each LoRA module consumes an identical number of trainable parameters, regardless of its potentially varying contributions to the overall model performance.
\item Current LoRA implementations predominantly adhere to the Transformer architecture. However, there has been limited exploration into extending the model architecture to enhance performance. This leads to the broader question of whether it is necessary to incorporate LoRA modules under these constraints and whether LoRA modules would be more effective with specific new connections, such as shortcut connections<|CIT-MASK|>.
\end{itemize}

In this work, we introduce \textit{HeteroLoRA}, a new lightweight framework designed to autonomously allocate the LoRA module across the entire LLM given a parameter budget. Furthermore, we perform HeteroLoRA within an expanded search space including LoRA-adapted shortcut connections<|CIT-MASK|> as illustrated in \Cref{fig: heterolora}.

Specifically, we make the following contributions:

\begin{itemize}
\item We propose HeteroLoRA, a novel LoRA configuration search algorithm to solve the rank allocation problem within a limited trainable parameter budget. HeteroLoRA leverages zero-cost proxies<|CIT-MASK|> to avoid the high cost of brute-force search.
\item We further prove the efficacy of the LoRA-adapted shortcut connection and combine it with HeteroLoRA to improve global synergies. The shortcuts suggested by HeteroLoRA enable more gains in model performance given the same parameter budget. For instance, on MRPC, we see an improvement of 1.6\% in accuracy with similar model size budgets.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{-1em}

\subsection{Low-Rank Adaptation}

Despite the scalability of fine-tuning over different downstream tasks, conducting a full fine-tuning on an LLM is often parameter-inefficient: every downstream task requires a large set of its own tuned parameters<|CIT-MASK|>. Therefore, a series of special fine-tuning strategies referred to as \textit{parameter-efficient tuning} (PET) was studied to resolve the issue.

PET adapters introduce a small set of trainable parameters. These parameters of the adapter modules are trained on each downstream task while the pre-trained model parameters remain fixed. Switching between model versions of different downstream tasks only requires changing the tuned adapter modules.

\textbf{Low-rank adaptation} (LoRA)<|CIT-MASK|> is an adapter method that exploits the intrinsic low-rank structures in deep learning. The LoRA module is placed directly on top of pre-trained matrices and thus can be merged during inference to avoid extra inference latency.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/lora_overview.pdf}
\caption{Overview of LoRA applied to a Transformer model. LoRA can be applied to each Transformer block. Taking a multi-head attention submodule in a block as an example, LoRA can be applied to linear projections transforming $Q$, $K$, $V$ and the concatenated output. For each LoRA module, the input is transformed by both a pre-trained weight $W$ and two low-rank matrices $A$ and $B$, whose results are added and returned.}
\label{fig: lora}
\end{figure}

Mathematically, LoRA hypothesises that the updates $\Delta W$ to the pre-trained weights during fine-tuning have a low ``intrinsic rank''. Consequently, the update can be represented as the product of two rectangular low-rank decomposition matrices, yielding:
$$W_0 + \Delta W = W_0 + BA$$
where $W_0\in \mathbb{R}^{d\times k}$ denotes the pre-trained matrix, $\Delta W\in \mathbb{R}^{d\times k}$ denotes the update, and $B\in \mathbb{R}^{d\times r}$ and $A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}$ with rank $r \ll \min(d,k)$ represents the low-rank decomposition matrices. By setting the LoRA rank $r$ to the rank of the pre-trained weight matrices, the expressiveness of full fine-tuning is roughly recovered by updating only the decomposition matrices. In other words, as $r$ increases, fine-tuning the LoRA-adapted model roughly converges to full fine-tuning on the original PLM<|CIT-MASK|>.

During fine-tuning, only the matrices $A$ and $B$ are unfrozen, yielding much less trainable parameters: $d\times r + r\times k = (d+k)\times r \ll d\times k$.

To avoid shifts of the variance in the matrix product for a similar reason as in the Scaled Dot-Product Attention, a scaling factor $\frac{\alpha}{r}$ is introduced to $BA$, where $\alpha$ is a pre-defined constant acting as a scaling on the learning rate over the LoRA modules.

In order to keep $\Delta W = 0$ at the beginning of the fine-tuning, the decomposition matrix $A$ is initialised by random Gaussian, while matrix $B$ is initialised to zeros.

In practice, LoRA can be applied to the Transformer architecture on several linear transformations, including the linear projections $W_i^Q$, $W_i^K$, $W_i^V$ and $W^O$ in the attention module and the two fully-connected layers $W_1$ and $W_2$ in the FFN module. For simplicity and parameter efficiency, the multi-head projections $W_i^Q$, $W_i^K$, $W_i^V$ are treated as single matrices $W^Q, W^K, W^V \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\mathrm{model}\times d_\mathrm{model}}$, and only a subset of linear transformations are adapted to LoRA.

\subsection{Shortcut Connections}
\label{sec: shortcut connections}

The residual connections in the Transformer architecture, as well as other shortcut connection architectures, have been well-explored and obtained significant performance.

The residual connection has been employed by \textbf{ResNet}<|CIT-MASK|> to image processing and achieved impressive results. The residual connection acts as an identity shortcut, taking the input to a function and adding it back to the function's output.

The significance of the residual connection lies in addressing the optimisation difficulty as model depth increases. As depth grows, accuracy degradation occurs beyond a certain threshold. ResNet proposed that deep models may struggle to approximate identity mappings effectively using multiple non-linear layers. To address this, ResNet introduced residual connections, enabling the model to learn residual mappings more efficiently. By fitting a residual mapping $\mathcal{F}(\mathbf{x}) \triangleq \mathcal{H}(\mathbf{x}) - \mathbf{x}$ to achieve the desired mapping $\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{x})$, the model becomes easier to optimise<|CIT-MASK|>.

Beyond the residual learning, shortcut connections have also been used to model certain kinds of \textit{inter-dependencies} between model blocks. In the \textbf{SENet}<|CIT-MASK|>, a ``squeeze-and-excitation'' operation is introduced after a block of transformations (e.g.\ convolutions) to model channel dependencies explicitly. The operation first ``squeezes'' the global spatial information by averaging across the $C$ channels; then ``excites'' the vector of averages using two fully connected layers to capture the inter-dependencies with $\mathbf{s}\in \mathbb{R}^C$; and finally rescales the original channels according to $\mathbf{s}$. This kind of information flow aids the SENet in achieving significant improvement across models with various depths.

\textbf{DenseNet}<|CIT-MASK|> offers an intuitive shortcut-adapted architecture. It divides a deep convolutional network into blocks based on feature map sizes. Within each block, the input to the current layer includes outputs from all preceding layers. Unlike ResNet's residual connections, DenseNet concatenates feature maps from previous layers rather than summing them, enhancing information flow. Consequently, DenseNet achieves the following: (a) forms a global state of "collective knowledge" accessible to any network layer; (b) promotes feature reuse, enhancing parameter efficiency; and (c) allows individual layers to receive additional supervision from the loss through shorter connections, addressing the vanishing-gradient problem and reinforcing feature propagation.

